```java
class Animal {
    static void identify() { System.out.println("Animal"); }
}
class Dog extends Animal {
    static void identify() { System.out.println("Dog"); }
}

Animal a = new Dog();
a.identify(); // prints "Animal" — resolved by the reference type (Animal),
              // NOT the actual object type (Dog)
```

If this were an instance method, `a.identify()` would print "Dog" — that's polymorphism. Because it's `static`, the compiler picks the method based on what the variable is *declared* as, not what it actually points to at runtime.
